/root/autodl-tmp/pacs/paper/methodology.tex:8:We evaluate all 500 problems from MATH-500~\cite{math500-neurips2021}. Each problem is decoded greedily (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048). Problems with degenerate phase assignments (null low or high phase) are excluded, yielding effective sample sizes of $n{=}496$ for DeepSeek (4 excluded) and $n{=}475$ for Qwen (25 excluded). Qwen exhibits a higher mid-phase null rate (45.2\% vs.\ 10.2\% for DeepSeek) due to lower temporal autocorrelation in its attention sparsity patterns (lag-1 $r{=}0.718$ vs.\ $0.894$, $d{=}-1.68$); this does not affect the primary gradient metric $\Delta = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low}} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high}}$, which requires only low and high phases. Sensitivity analysis across $\texttt{min\_phase\_length} \in \{5, 10, 20\}$ supports robustness (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}).
/root/autodl-tmp/pacs/paper/methodology.tex:18:All reported confidence intervals use nonparametric bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$, seed$=$42)~\cite{efron-1993}. Significance tests use Wilcoxon signed-rank (paired within-model) or Mann-Whitney $U$ (cross-training-paradigm), with Bonferroni correction at family-wise $k{=}4$ (per model$\times$strategy combination, 4 sparsity levels testing one dose-response hypothesis): each family tests a single directional question (``does $\Delta$ increase with $s$?'') under fixed model and strategy, making $k{=}4$ the natural grouping. Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction across all 44 tests is reported as a robustness check in Appendix~\ref{app:bh-fdr}; all Bonferroni-significant results remain significant under BH-FDR. Dose-response monotonicity is supported by the Jonckheere-Terpstra test ($z{=}6.2$--$33.7$, all $p < 10^{-9}$), which uses per-problem values (${\sim}496$ per group) rather than aggregated medians.\footnote{Spearman rank correlation on the four aggregate medians ($\rho{=}1.0$, $n{=}4$; interpret with caution given the small number of levels) is consistent; we rely on the JT test as primary evidence.} Scaling from $n{=}50$ (profiling) to $n{=}500$ narrowed bootstrap CI widths by $2.5$--$5.9{\times}$ across all 16 conditions, showing precision gains. The paired median difference $\Delta$ is the primary metric; $G$ (amplification ratio, Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}) is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:g-metric} as an auxiliary illustrative ratio.\footnote{Experiments on 4$\times$ professional GPUs with 48\,GB memory.}
